1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,108
Lesson 7.6.

2
00:00:02,488 --> 00:00:05,084
Physical realization with RC networks.

3
00:00:06,217 --> 00:00:09,741
The theorem of lesson
7.4 assumes a physical

4
00:00:09,741 --> 00:00:14,066
diffuser: a device that
averages at all nodes at once.

5
00:00:14,446 --> 00:00:17,350
The most natural
realization is a network of

6
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:20,849
resistors and capacitors,
like the one in lesson 5.5.

7
00:00:21,229 --> 00:00:25,088
Today we see how well it works and,
above all, how much it really costs.

8
00:00:26,217 --> 00:00:29,399
Each node of the Cayley
graph is a capacitor C_0

9
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:32,316
to ground, and each
edge is a conductance g.

10
00:00:32,696 --> 00:00:37,526
Kirchhoff's laws give C_0
V̇ = −L V, with L the graph

11
00:00:37,526 --> 00:00:42,812
Laplacian: voltage diffuses
like heat, in continuous time.

12
00:00:43,192 --> 00:00:47,895
Injecting charge at the identity,
the normalized voltage there

13
00:00:47,895 --> 00:00:52,598
approaches one over r with an
error smaller than e^(−2gt/C_0),

14
00:00:52,598 --> 00:00:56,163
exactly, without
discretization approximations.

15
00:00:56,543 --> 00:01:01,032
For r = 33, after one time
unit the actual error is

16
00:01:01,032 --> 00:01:05,170
five ten-millionths,
against the bound of 0.14.

17
00:01:06,283 --> 00:01:08,067
The first cost is nodes.

18
00:01:08,447 --> 00:01:11,580
The network needs at
least r nodes, one per

19
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:14,786
group element, and r
can be comparable to N.

20
00:01:15,166 --> 00:01:19,552
Worse: building a cycle of
r nodes assumes knowing r.

21
00:01:19,932 --> 00:01:24,626
The honest solution uses one
address per residue modulo N, that

22
00:01:24,626 --> 00:01:28,723
is, N nodes, and lets the
charge occupy only the orbit.

23
00:01:29,103 --> 00:01:31,968
For a two-thousand-forty-eight-bit
number, that is a

24
00:01:31,968 --> 00:01:34,723
number of nodes with more
than six hundred digits.

25
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:37,897
The second cost is precision.

26
00:01:38,277 --> 00:01:42,328
The readings for neighboring
orders, one over r

27
00:01:42,328 --> 00:01:46,035
and one over r + 1,
differ by 1/(r(r + 1)).

28
00:01:46,415 --> 00:01:51,225
To distinguish orders up
to B, absolute precision

29
00:01:51,225 --> 00:01:55,643
of order 1/B² is needed:
about 2 log₂ B bits.

30
00:01:56,023 --> 00:01:58,423
For thirty-three that
is ten bits; for five

31
00:01:58,423 --> 00:02:00,935
thousand three hundred
thirteen, twenty-five.

32
00:02:01,315 --> 00:02:04,429
For a cryptographic number
it would be thousands of

33
00:02:04,429 --> 00:02:07,176
analog bits: something
no voltmeter achieves.

34
00:02:08,283 --> 00:02:11,445
Unequal conductances
only change the speed;

35
00:02:11,445 --> 00:02:14,166
unequal capacitors change the result.

36
00:02:14,546 --> 00:02:19,825
At equilibrium, the voltage at
the identity is its capacitance

37
00:02:19,825 --> 00:02:25,274
divided by the total sum, so the
reading is r(1 + ε̄)/(1 + ε_e).

38
00:02:25,654 --> 00:02:29,974
To round correctly one needs
∣ε̄ − ε_e∣ < (1 + ε_e)/(2r).

39
00:02:30,354 --> 00:02:34,309
With r = 33 and ten thousand
networks with random

40
00:02:34,309 --> 00:02:39,231
mismatches, half a percent is
right 99.6 percent of the time,

41
00:02:39,611 --> 00:02:44,092
one percent 88 percent, and
three percent only 40 percent.

42
00:02:45,217 --> 00:02:47,972
Finally, the thermal noise of the readout.

43
00:02:48,352 --> 00:02:53,226
Sampling with a capacitor C
has a variance of k_B T/C.

44
00:02:53,606 --> 00:02:57,032
To reach the required
precision many samples must be

45
00:02:57,032 --> 00:03:00,986
averaged: with one picofarad,
one volt and room temperature,

46
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:05,106
for orders of a thousand about
two hundred sixty-five thousand

47
00:03:05,106 --> 00:03:08,786
samples are needed; for a million,
on the order of ten to the

48
00:03:08,786 --> 00:03:11,743
seventeen; for a billion,
ten to the twenty-nine.

49
00:03:12,123 --> 00:03:15,820
These are numbers from a simple
model, not universal bounds, but they

50
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:19,357
show that counting cells is not
enough to demonstrate feasibility.

51
00:03:20,483 --> 00:03:24,585
An honest prototype must
reproduce the exact heat kernel in

52
00:03:24,585 --> 00:03:28,965
small cases, calibrate the
capacitors below one over two r, and

53
00:03:28,965 --> 00:03:32,858
report nodes, bits of precision
and samples per readout.

54
00:03:33,238 --> 00:03:36,808
In project five you will simulate
the network with mismatch and noise.

55
00:03:37,917 --> 00:03:42,122
Common mistakes: counting diffusion
steps and ignoring the nodes.

56
00:03:42,502 --> 00:03:46,171
Building a cycle of length
r, which assumes the answer.

57
00:03:46,551 --> 00:03:51,333
Believing that two log₂ B bits are
cheap, when they are analog precision.

58
00:03:51,713 --> 00:03:55,673
And calibrating conductances
while ignoring capacitor mismatch.

59
00:03:56,783 --> 00:04:01,198
In summary: an RC network
realizes diffusion

60
00:04:01,198 --> 00:04:05,712
exactly, with error
decaying as e^(−2gt/C_0).

61
00:04:06,092 --> 00:04:10,264
But it needs on the order of r
nodes, about two log₂ B bits of

62
00:04:10,264 --> 00:04:14,503
precision, capacitors matched
better than one over two r, and a

63
00:04:14,503 --> 00:04:18,472
number of samples growing as
the fourth power of the order.

64
00:04:18,852 --> 00:04:22,045
Time is short; the cost
has moved to the hardware.

65
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:27,460
To practice: derive the equilibrium
reading with charge conservation.

66
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:30,472
Compute the bits for
orders up to a million.

67
00:04:30,852 --> 00:04:34,044
Compute the mismatch tolerance
for an order of a thousand.

68
00:04:34,424 --> 00:04:37,325
And explain why one address
per residue is needed.
